    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizu-of-liao">Abaoji</a> united the Khitan tribes and proclaimed himself emperor in 916; the state took the dynastic name Liao in 947.</li><li>It ruled nomads and Chinese farmers through a dual system of Northern and Southern Administrations.</li><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanyuan-treaty">Treaty of Chanyuan</a> of 1005 brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchen</a> Jin destroyed the Liao by 1125.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cathay">Cathay</a>, the medieval European name for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and Kitai, its Russian name, both come from the Khitan, the people who ruled the Liao empire.</p></aside>
    </section>

