    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/shi-jingtang"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shi-jingtang.jpg" alt="Shi Jingtang" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/shi-jingtang">Shi Jingtang</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shi_Jingtang_(Jin_Gaozu).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Khitan were a Mongolic-speaking people of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xar-moron-river">Xar Moron</a> and Liao river basins in what is now eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inner-mongolia">Inner Mongolia</a>, organised in a confederation of eight tribes whose leaders were chosen for fixed terms. For centuries they had alternated between submission to and raids on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a>, the Turks and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghurs</a>. Traditionally in 907, as the Tang dynasty collapsed, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizu-of-liao">Abaoji</a> of the Yila tribe and the Yelü clan was chosen as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">khagan</a>. Drawing on a personal guard, the wealth of Chinese captives and settlers and the support of his wife's Shulü clan, he refused to give up office, crushed revolts by his own brothers between 911 and 913, and in 916 proclaimed himself emperor with a Chinese-style reign title, naming his eldest son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yelu-bei">Yelü Bei</a> as heir. He built a walled capital, later called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shangjing-of-liao">Shangjing</a>, in 918, campaigned west into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> in 924 and in 926 destroyed Balhae, turning it into the client kingdom of Dongdan. He died on the way home. His widow, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shulu-ping">Empress Shulü</a> Ping, secured the throne for their second son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yelu-deguang">Yelü Deguang</a> (Emperor Taizong, r. 927–947), who in 936 helped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shi-jingtang">Shi Jingtang</a> found the Later Jin dynasty in north <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and received in return the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sixteen-prefectures">Sixteen Prefectures</a> around modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/datong">Datong</a>. In 947 the state adopted the dynastic name Liao, which later alternated with the name Khitan.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-of-ghazni"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mahmud-of-ghazni.jpg" alt="Mahmud of Ghazni" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-of-ghazni">Mahmud of Ghazni</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mahmud_of_Ghazni,_Ghaznavid_ruler,_conquering_Qasdar_(modern_Khuzdar)_in_India,_miniature_from_the_Jami%CA%BF_al-Tawarikh_of_Rashid_al-Din_Il-Khanid_Tabriz_Ms_Or_20_f.108v.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1314-15, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Liao empire united very different peoples and economies. In the north the Khitan and other nomads, such as the Xi and the tribes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>, herded horses, sheep and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and hunted, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchen</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> fished, farmed and traded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a>, while in the south the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sixteen-prefectures">Sixteen Prefectures</a> and the Liao valley held a large population of Chinese farmers, along with Balhae settlers who grew grain and worked iron. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizu-of-liao">Abaoji</a> and his successors also settled captured Chinese and Balhae artisans and farmers in walled towns on the steppe, creating an agricultural and craft economy inside the Khitan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">homeland</a>. The annual payments of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a> after 1005, together with frontier markets where horses, sheep and furs were exchanged for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a>, textiles and grain, gave the court great wealth. Liao merchants traded with Goryeo, the Western Xia and the oases of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, and a Khitan embassy reached the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-of-ghazni">Mahmud of Ghazni</a> in 1026–1027. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> flourished under imperial patronage, especially under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shengzong-of-liao">Shengzong</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-daozong-of-liao">Daozong</a>; monasteries held great estates, and the Liao printed their own edition of the Buddhist canon. Khitan elites also kept their own customs, including sacrifices to Heaven and at the sacred Muye mountain, and elaborate burials such as the tomb of the Princess of Chen (1018), whose occupants wore gold face masks.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/xiao-yanyan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/xiao-yanyan.jpg" alt="Xiao Yanyan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/xiao-yanyan">Xiao Yanyan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Empress_Dowager_Xiao_1892.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">熊大木, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Liao army was built around Khitan cavalry. Each emperor maintained an <em>ordo</em>, a mobile camp with its own guard, households and estates, which continued after his death to supply troops and to serve his tomb, while the tribes provided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mounted-archer">mounted archers</a> led by Khitan nobles. Chinese and Balhae subjects supplied infantry, engineers and siege specialists, and fortified towns guarded the frontiers. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yelu-deguang">Yelü Deguang</a> the army reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a>: in 946–947 he overthrew the Later Jin, whose second ruler had defied him, and briefly ruled north <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> before revolts forced him to withdraw; he died on the march north. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a> reunified China it tried to recover the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sixteen-prefectures">Sixteen Prefectures</a>, but Liao forces defeated the Song emperor Taizong at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-gaoliang-river">Gaoliang River</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> in 979 and repelled a three-pronged Song invasion in 986. In 1004 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiao-yanyan">Empress Dowager Chengtian</a> and Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shengzong-of-liao">Shengzong</a> led an army deep into Song territory to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanyuan-treaty">Chanyuan</a>, near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a>, forcing the treaty that brought more than a century of peace. The Liao fought three wars with Goryeo, burning its capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaesong">Kaegyong</a> in 1011, but suffered a heavy defeat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-gwiju">Battle of Gwiju</a> in 1019, after which the two states made peace. A Liao campaign against the Western Xia in 1044 also ended in failure.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/liaoyang"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/liaoyang.jpg" alt="Liaoyang" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/liaoyang">Liaoyang</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E8%BE%BD%E9%98%B3%E7%99%BD%E5%A1%94%E5%85%AC%E5%9B%AD09.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">红烧腰果, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Liao state is best known for its dual administration, which governed nomads and farmers by different methods. The Northern Administration (<em>beimian</em>), staffed mainly by Khitan nobles, ran military affairs and the tribes under Khitan customary law, while the Southern Administration (<em>nanmian</em>) governed the Chinese and Balhae population through Tang-style offices, prefectures and counties and, from the late tenth century, recruited Chinese officials partly through civil service examinations. The empire had five capitals: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shangjing-of-liao">Shangjing</a> in the Khitan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">homeland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaoyang">Dongjing</a> at Liaoyang, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a> at modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, Zhongjing near Ningcheng and Xijing at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/datong">Datong</a>. Yet the real centre of government was mobile, as the court moved between seasonal camps (<em>nabo</em>) for hunting, fishing and councils in an annual cycle. Power was shared between the imperial Yelü clan and the consort Xiao clan, which supplied the empresses, and strong empresses and empresses dowager often governed, notably the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiao-yanyan">Empress Dowager Chengtian</a>, regent for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shengzong-of-liao">Shengzong</a> from 982 until her death in 1009. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizu-of-liao">Abaoji</a>'s court created two Khitan scripts, a large script in 920 and a small script a few years later, which were used for inscriptions and documents alongside Chinese and are still only partly deciphered. Law also ran on two tracks, with different rules and penalties for Khitan and Chinese, which Shengzong began to harmonise.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/dule-temple"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dule-temple.jpg" alt="Dule Temple" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/dule-temple">Dule Temple</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guanyin_Pavilion_of_Dule_Temple.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Xin, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For two centuries the Liao was the strongest power in north-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, and its relationship with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a> set a pattern for East Asian diplomacy. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanyuan-treaty">Treaty of Chanyuan</a> of 1005 treated the two emperors as equals who addressed each other as elder and younger brothers, and the peace it established lasted more than a century, showing that a Chinese dynasty could coexist with a steppe empire on equal terms. The Liao was the first of the so-called conquest dynasties, and its combination of steppe and Chinese government influenced the Jin dynasty of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchen</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Mongol Yuan</a> and, through them, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Manchu Qing</a>. Its southern capital at modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> began that city's rise to an imperial capital, completed by the Jin and the Yuan. Liao Buddhist architecture survives in some of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s oldest wooden buildings, including the Guanyin Pavilion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dule-temple">Dule Temple</a>, built in 984, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pagoda-of-fogong-temple">Sakyamuni Pagoda of Fogong Temple</a> at Yingxian, completed in 1056 and the oldest surviving all-wooden pagoda in China. Because the Khitan dominated northern China, Central Asian and later European writers named the region after them: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cathay">Khitay</a> in Persian, Kitai in Russian and Cathay in medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, a name popularised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo">Marco Polo</a> and other travellers.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizu-of-jin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emperor-taizu-of-jin.jpg" alt="Emperor Taizu of Jin" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizu-of-jin">Emperor Taizu of Jin</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wanggiyan_Aguda.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Liao entered the twelfth century with its institutions intact but its leadership weakened. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-daozong-of-liao">Daozong</a> (r. 1055–1101) factional struggles at court led to the forced suicide of the empress in 1075 and the murder of the crown prince in 1077, plots blamed on the minister Yelü Yixin, while heavy spending on Buddhist foundations strained the treasury. The last emperor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-tianzuo-of-liao">Tianzuo</a> (r. 1101–1125), devoted himself to hunting and alienated subject peoples by demanding tribute such as gyrfalcons and pearls from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchen</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a>. In 1114 the Jurchen chieftain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizu-of-jin">Wanyan Aguda</a> rebelled and defeated Liao forces at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ningjiang">Ningjiang</a>, and in 1115 he proclaimed the Jin dynasty; later that year his small army routed a far larger force led by Tianzuo at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hubudagang">Hubudagang</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>, hoping to recover the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sixteen-prefectures">Sixteen Prefectures</a>, allied with the Jin in 1120, and the Liao capitals fell one by one: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaoyang">Dongjing</a> in 1116, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shangjing-of-liao">Shangjing</a> in 1120, and Zhongjing and Xijing in 1122. Princes at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a> set up a rival regime, Northern Liao, but the Jin took the city at the end of 1122. Tianzuo fled into the western mountains and was captured in 1125. The prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yelu-dashi">Yelü Dashi</a> had already led a band of followers west in 1124, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> he founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qara-khitai">Qara Khitai</a>, or Western Liao, which lasted until 1218.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Liao dynasty of the Khitan ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> and the far north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> for two centuries, pioneering a dual administration for nomads and farmers that later conquest dynasties followed, holding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> to a treaty of equals at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanyuan-treaty">Chanyuan</a> and giving China its medieval European name, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cathay">Cathay</a>.</em></p>

